------------\nHtmlSanitizer UrlAttributeSanitizer Omits action/formaction/poster/cite: javascript: URI Survives Sanitization (XSS) · Advisory · symfony/symfony · GitHub (https://github.com/symfony/symfony/security/advisories/GHSA-hhg7-c65m-h7ff)\n【turn957545search1】 [wordlim: 200] Published: 5 months ago; Crawled: last week; # HtmlSanitizer UrlAttributeSanitizer Omits action/formaction/poster/cite: javascript: URI Survives Sanitization (XSS) ... symfony/html-sanitizer (Composer)\n\n# HtmlSanitizer UrlAttributeSanitizer Omits action/formaction/poster/cite: javascript: URI Survives Sanitization (XSS)\n\nLow\n\nnicolas-grekas published GHSA-hhg7-c65m-h7ff May 20, 2026\n\n## Package\n\nsymfony/html-sanitizer (Composer)\n\n## Affected versions\n\n>=6.1, <6.4.40\n\n>=7, <7.4.12\n\n>=8, <8.0.12\n\n## Patched versions\n\n6.4.40\n\n7.4.12\n\n8.0.12\n\nsymfony/symfony (Composer)\n\n>=6.1, <6.4.40\n\n>=7, <7.4.12\n\n>=8, <8.0.12\n\n6.4.40\n\n7.4.12\n\n8.0.12\n\n## Description\n\n### Description\n\n`symfony/html-sanitizer` lets applications sanitise untrusted HTML. `UrlAttributeSanitizer` is the visitor responsible for validating URL-valued attributes and stripping dangerous schemes from them; it runs on every element regardless of configuration. Whether an attribute is kept is decided by the element/attribute allow-list; validating the scheme of a URL attribute is solely `UrlAttributeSanitizer`'s responsibility.\n\n`UrlAttributeSanitizer::getSupportedAttributes()` returned only `['src', 'href', 'lowsrc', 'background', 'ping']`. The HTML URL-valued attributes `action` (`<form>`), `formaction` (`<button>`, `<input type=image>`), `poster` (`<video>`) and `cite` (`<blockquote>`, `<q>`, `<del>`, `<ins>`) were missing from that list, so `DomVisitor` never invoked scheme validation for them. As a result, when a configuration admits one of those attributes, a `javascript:` URI in it survived sanitisation.\n\n### Conditions for exploitation\n\n`allowSafeElements()` is not affected: `<form>` and the `formaction` attribute are both flagged unsafe in `W3CReference`, and `allowElement('form')` resets the element's attribute list. Reaching the vulnerable attributes requires a deliberately permissive configuration, for example:\n\n  * `<form>` + `action`: `allowElement('form', '*')`, `allowElement('form', ['action', …])`, `allowElement('form')->allowAttribute('action', 'form')`, or the `allowStaticElements()` preset (whose docblock already warns the output \"may still contain other dangerous behaviors\");\n  * `<button>` / `<input type=image>` + `formaction`: `allowElement(…, '*')`, `allowAttribute('formaction', …)`, or `allowStaticElements()`;\n  * `<blockquote>` / `<q>` / `<del>` / `<ins>` + `cite`, or `<video>` + `poster`: similarly via `'*'`, `allowAttribute()`, or `allowStaticElements()`.\n\nFor the `action` / `formaction` cases the victim must additionally submit the form or click the button.\n\n### Resolution\n\n`UrlAttributeSanitizer` now also handles `action`, `formaction`, `cite` and `poster`. `action` / `formaction` / `cite` are validated against the link schemes (like `<a href>`, so `javascript:` is rejected and `data:` is dropped too); `poster` is validated against the media schemes (so `data:` images keep working). The behaviour of `<a href>` and `<img src>` is unchanged.\n\nOne behaviour change to be aware of: a relative `action=\"/submit\"` on an allowed `<form>` is now dropped by default (the same as `<a href>` / `<img src>` today); `->allowRelativeLinks()` re-enables it.\n\nThe patch for this issue is available here for branch 6.4.\n\n### Credits\n\nWe would like to thank Himanshu Anand and Rémi Pelloux for reporting the issue and Nicolas Grekas for providing the fix.\n\n### Severity\n\nLow\n\n### CVE ID\n\nCVE-2026-45753\n\n### Weaknesses\n\nWeakness CWE-79\n\n#### Improper Neutralization of Input During Web Page Generation ('Cross-site Scripting')\n\nThe product does not neutralize or incorrectly neutralizes user-controllable input before it is placed in output that is used as a web page that is served to other users. Learn more on MITRE.\n\nWeakness CWE-184\n\n#### Incomplete List of Disallowed Inputs\n\nThe product implements a protection mechanism that relies on a list of inputs (or properties of inputs) that are not allowed by policy or otherwise require other action to neutralize before additional processing takes place, but the list is incomplete. Learn more on MITRE.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSecurity Advisories · rails/rails-html-sanitizer · GitHub (https://github.com/rails/rails-html-sanitizer/security/advisories)\n【turn957545search2】 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; ## Security Advisories ...   * Possible XSS vulnerability with certain configurations of rails-html-sanitizer\n\nrails / rails-html-sanitizer Public\n\n  * Notifications You must be signed in to change notification settings\n  * Fork 85\n  * Star\n\n  * Code\n  * Issues 6\n  * Pull requests 3\n  * Actions\n  * Security and quality 10\n  * Insights\n\nAdditional navigation options\n\n# Security: rails/rails-html-sanitizer\n\n## Security and quality\n\n  * Overview\n  *   * ### Reporting\n\n    * Security policy\n    * Advisories\n\n## Security Advisories\n\nView information about security vulnerabilities from this repository's maintainers.\n\n[Input: Filter advisories: severity:critical, ecosystem:npm]\n\n  * Possible XSS vulnerability with certain configurations of rails-html-sanitizer\n\nGHSA-cj75-f6xr-r4g7 published Jul 16, 2026 by flavorjones\n\nModerate\n\n  * Possible XSS vulnerability with certain configurations of rails-html-sanitizer 1.6.0\n\nGHSA-rxv5-gxqc-xx8g published Dec 2, 2024 by flavorjones\n\nModerate\n\n  * Possible XSS vulnerability with certain configurations of rails-html-sanitizer 1.6.0\n\nGHSA-cfjx-w229-hgx5 published Dec 2, 2024 by flavorjones\n\nModerate\n\n  * Possible XSS vulnerability with certain configurations of rails-html-sanitizer 1.6.0\n\nGHSA-638j-pmjw-jq48 published Dec 2, 2024 by flavorjones\n\nModerate\n\n  * Possible XSS vulnerability with certain configurations of rails-html-sanitizer 1.6.0\n\nGHSA-2x5m-9ch4-qgrr published Dec 2, 2024 by flavorjones\n\nModerate\n\n  * Possible XSS vulnerability with certain configurations of rails-html-sanitizer 1.6.0\n\nGHSA-w8gc-x259-rc7x published Dec 2, 2024 by flavorjones\n\nModerate\n\n  * Possible XSS vulnerability with certain configurations of rails-html-sanitizer\n\nGHSA-9h9g-93gc-623h published Dec 13, 2022 by flavorjones\n\nModerate\n\n  * Possible XSS vulnerability with certain configurations of rails-html-sanitizer\n\nGHSA-rrfc-7g8p-99q8 published Dec 13, 2022 by flavorjones\n\nModerate\n\n  * Improper neutralization of data URIs may allow XSS in rails-html-sanitizer\n\nGHSA-mcvf-2q2m-x72m published Dec 13, 2022 by flavorjones\n\nModerate\n\n  * Inefficient Regular Expression Complexity in rails-html-sanitizer\n\nGHSA-5x79-w82f-gw8w published Dec 13, 2022 by flavorjones\n\nHigh\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nStored XSS: xss.RichText sanitize-then-unescape lets a low-privileged user persist live HTML that executes in the Calendar block's innerHTML sink · Advisory · cortezaproject/corteza · GitHub (https://github.com/cortezaproject/corteza/security/advisories/GHSA-j8cx-q7xw-8r7f)\n【turn957545search3】 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2 weeks ago; Crawled: last week; Compose record string values are passed through this function (`sanitizer.RunXSS -> sString -> xss.RichText`). ... No Content-Security-Policy: the official `cortezaproject/corteza-server:2024.9.10` image returns no `Content-Security-Policy`, `X-Frame-Options`, `X-Content-Type-Options`, or `X-XSS-Protection` header on the webapp or API responses, so inline `onerror=` handlers execute. ... Additionally, the Calendar block could set the title with `textContent` (or sanitize client-side at the sink) as defense in depth, and the server could emit a restrictive Content-Security-Policy. ... Attack vector: More severe the more the remote (logically and physically) an attacker can be in order to exploit the vulnerability.\n\n# Stored XSS: xss.RichText sanitize-then-unescape lets a low-privileged user persist live HTML that executes in the Calendar block's innerHTML sink\n\nThis report was submitted with AI assistance\n\nHigh\n\nFajfa published GHSA-j8cx-q7xw-8r7f Oct 19, 2026\n\n## Package\n\ngithub.com/cortezaproject/corteza/server (Go)\n\n## Affected versions\n\n>= 2022.3.0, < 2026.9.0\n\n## Patched versions\n\n2026.9.0\n\n## Description\n\n### Summary\n\n`xss.RichText` sanitizes its input with bluemonday and then calls `html.UnescapeString` on the entire sanitized string. Unescaping after sanitizing re-creates live markup that the sanitizer already neutralized: bluemonday emits dangerous text as HTML entities (e.g. `&lt;img ...&gt;`), and the trailing `html.UnescapeString` turns those entities back into real `<img ...>` tags that are never re-sanitized.\n\nCompose record string values are passed through this function (`sanitizer.RunXSS -> sString -> xss.RichText`). Each `RichText` pass removes exactly one layer of entity encoding, so a sufficiently multiply-encoded payload survives the whole write pipeline and is stored as a live tag. Step 4 — The attacker stores the triple-encoded payload. The server persists it as a live `<img onerror>`:\n    \n    # payload has no inner quotes and no spaces so it stays a valid unquoted HTML attribute AND valid JSON\n    printf '{\"values\":[{\"name\":\"Title\",\"value\":\"&amp;amp;lt;img src=x onerror=alert(window.__xss=document.domain)&amp;amp;gt;\"},{\"name\":\"Start\",\"value\":\"2026-10-19T10:00:00Z\"}]}' > rec.json\n    RID=$(curl -sS -X POST \"$B/namespace/$NID/module/$MID/record/\" -H \"Authorization: Bearer $ATTACKER\" -H 'Content-Type: application/json' --data-binary @rec.json | grep -oE '\"recordID\":\"[0-9]+\"' | grep -oE '[0-9]+')\n    \n    # Read it back; the stored Title is a live tag (note < = '<', > = '>'):\n    curl -sS \"$B/namespace/$NID/module/$MID/record/$RID\" -H \"Authorization: Bearer $ADMIN\"\n    # ... \"name\":\"Title\",\"value\":\"<img src=x onerror=alert(window.__xss=document.domain)>\" ...\n\nControls on the same module confirm the sanitizer works for naive input and is bypassed only by the encoding: a raw `<img src=x onerror=alert(1)>` and a single-encoded `&lt;img ... onerror ...&gt;` are both stored as `<img src=\"x\">` (handler stripped), whereas the triple-encoded value above is stored with the `onerror` handler intact.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nStored XSS in wysiwyg template fields if CSP is disabled · Advisory · espocrm/espocrm · GitHub (https://github.com/espocrm/espocrm/security/advisories/GHSA-x5p5-8mxj-9wv6)\n【turn957545search4】 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3 months ago; Crawled: 2 weeks ago; EspoCRM's client-side HTML sanitizer (moderateSanitizeHtml()) can be bypassed using crafted HTML, allowing users with Email Template or PDF Template edit permissions to store malicious JavaScript. ... If Content Security Policy is disabled (clientCspDisabled = true), this leads to stored XSS and can result in full session hijacking. ... Attack vector\n\n# Stored XSS in wysiwyg template fields if CSP is disabled\n\nLow\n\nyurikuzn published GHSA-x5p5-8mxj-9wv6 Jul 14, 2026\n\n## Package\n\nespocrm/espocrm (Composer)\n\n## Affected versions\n\n<= 9.3.8\n\n## Patched versions\n\n9.3.9\n\n## Description\n\n### Summary\n\nEspoCRM's client-side HTML sanitizer (moderateSanitizeHtml()) can be bypassed using crafted HTML, allowing users with Email Template or PDF Template edit permissions to store malicious JavaScript. When another user opens the template in edit mode, the payload executes. If Content Security Policy is disabled (clientCspDisabled = true), this leads to stored XSS and can result in full session hijacking.\n\nNote that by default CSP is enabled, making this vulnerability much less likely to be exploited.\n\n### Severity\n\nLow\n\n3.5\n\n# CVSS overall score\n\n#### CVSS v3 base metrics\n\nAttack vector\n\nNetwork\n\nAttack complexity\n\nLow\n\nPrivileges required\n\nLow\n\nUser interaction\n\nRequired\n\nScope\n\nUnchanged\n\nConfidentiality\n\nLow\n\nIntegrity\n\nNone\n\nAvailability\n\nNone\n\nCVSS:3.1/AV:N/AC:L/PR:L/UI:R/S:U/C:L/I:N/A:N\n\n### CVE ID\n\nCVE-2026-55184\n\n### Weaknesses\n\nWeakness CWE-79\n\n#### Improper Neutralization of Input During Web Page Generation ('Cross-site Scripting')\n\nThe product does not neutralize or incorrectly neutralizes user-controllable input before it is placed in output that is used as a web page that is served to other users. Learn more on MITRE.\n\n### Credits\n\n  * EvidentObscurity Reporter\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nDefault XSS via `xmp` raw-text passthrough in `sanitize-html` · Advisory · apostrophecms/apostrophe · GitHub (https://github.com/apostrophecms/apostrophe/security/advisories/GHSA-rpr9-rxv7-x643)\n【turn957545search5】 [wordlim: 200] Published: 5 months ago; Crawled: 3 weeks ago; This is a sanitizer bypass in the default `disallowedTagsMode: 'discard'` path and can lead to stored XSS in applications that render sanitized output back to users. ... This is a cross-site scripting vulnerability in the default sanitizer behavior.\n\n# Default XSS via `xmp` raw-text passthrough in `sanitize-html`\n\nCritical\n\nboutell published GHSA-rpr9-rxv7-x643 May 13, 2026\n\n## Package\n\nsanitize-html (npm)\n\n## Affected versions\n\n<= 2.17.3\n\n## Patched versions\n\nnone\n\n## Description\n\n### Summary\n\nUnder the default configuration, `sanitize-html` can turn attacker-controlled content inside a disallowed `xmp` element into live HTML or JavaScript. This is a sanitizer bypass in the default `disallowedTagsMode: 'discard'` path and can lead to stored XSS in applications that render sanitized output back to users.\n\n### Details\n\nIn `sanitize-html@2.17.3`, the default `nonTextTags` list includes only `script`, `style`, `textarea`, and `option` in `index.js` lines 138-142. That means disallowed `xmp` tags are not treated as \"drop the entire contents\" tags.\n\nLater, in the `ontext` handler at `index.js` lines 569-577, the code special-cases `textarea` and `xmp` and appends their text content directly to the output without escaping:\n    \n    } else if ((options.disallowedTagsMode === 'discard' || options.disallowedTagsMode === 'completelyDiscard') && (tag === 'textarea' || tag === 'xmp')) {\n\n  3. Observed output:\n    \n    <script>alert(1)</script>\n    <img src=x onerror=alert(1)>\n    <svg><script>alert(1)</script></svg>\n\n  4. Render any of the returned strings in a browser context that trusts `sanitize-html` output, for example:\n    \n    const dirty = '<xmp><script>alert(1)</script></xmp>';\n    const clean = sanitizeHtml(dirty);\n\nIf `clean` is inserted into the DOM or stored and later rendered as trusted HTML, the attacker-controlled script executes.\n\n### Impact\n\nThis is a cross-site scripting vulnerability in the default sanitizer behavior. Any application that uses `sanitize-html` defaults and then renders the returned HTML as trusted output is impacted. A remote attacker who can submit HTML content can trigger execution of arbitrary JavaScript in another user's browser when that content is viewed.\n\n### Severity\n\nCritical\n\n9.3\n\n# CVSS overall score\n\nThis score calculates overall vulnerability severity from 0 to 10 and is based on the Common Vulnerability Scoring System (CVSS).\n\n#### CVSS v3 base metrics\n\nAttack vector\n\nNetwork\n\nAttack complexity\n\nLow\n\nPrivileges required\n\nNone\n\nUser interaction\n\nRequired\n\nScope\n\nChanged\n\nConfidentiality\n\nHigh\n\nIntegrity\n\nHigh\n\nAvailability\n\nNone\n\nCVSS:3.1/AV:N/AC:L/PR:N/UI:R/S:C/C:H/I:H/A:N\n\n### CVE ID\n\nCVE-2026-44990\n\n### Weaknesses\n\nWeakness CWE-79\n\n#### Improper Neutralization of Input During Web Page Generation ('Cross-site Scripting')\n\nThe product does not neutralize or incorrectly neutralizes user-controllable input before it is placed in output that is used as a web page that is served to other users. Learn more on MITRE.\n\n### Credits\n\n  * sushi-gif Reporter\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nTwo-Way Property Binding Sanitization Bypass (XSS) · Advisory · angular/angular · GitHub (https://github.com/angular/angular/security/advisories/GHSA-58w9-8g37-x9v5)\n【turn957545search6】 [wordlim: 200] Published: 4 months ago; Crawled: last month; Specifically, when a native DOM property that requires sanitization (such as `innerHTML`, `srcdoc`, `src`, `href`, `data`, or `sandbox`) is bound using the two-way binding syntax (e.g., `[(innerHTML)]=\"value\"` or `bindon-innerHTML=\"value\"`), the Angular template compiler failed to apply the appropriate schema-derived sanitizer resolution to the `TwoWayProperty` operation. ... This flaw enables an attacker who can control the value of a two-way bound sensitive property to bypass Angular's built-in sanitization logic, potentially leading to client-side Cross-Site Scripting (XSS). ... Attack Complexity: This metric captures measurable actions that must be taken by the attacker to actively evade or circumvent existing built-in security-enhancing conditions in order to obtain a working exploit.\n\n# Two-Way Property Binding Sanitization Bypass (XSS)\n\nModerate\n\nalan-agius4 published GHSA-58w9-8g37-x9v5 Jun 10, 2026\n\n## Package\n\n@angular/compiler (npm)\n\n## Affected versions\n\n>= 22.0.0-next.0 < 22.0.1\n\n>= 21.0.0-next.0 < 21.2.17\n\n>= 20.0.0-next.0 < 20.3.25\n\n<= 19.2.25\n\n## Patched versions\n\n22.0.1\n\n21.2.17\n\n20.3.25\n\nnone\n\n## Description\n\nAn issue in the `@angular/compiler` package allows bypassing DOM property sanitization through the use of two-way property bindings.\n\nSpecifically, when a native DOM property that requires sanitization (such as `innerHTML`, `srcdoc`, `src`, `href`, `data`, or `sandbox`) is bound using the two-way binding syntax (e.g., `[(innerHTML)]=\"value\"` or `bindon-innerHTML=\"value\"`), the Angular template compiler failed to apply the appropriate schema-derived sanitizer resolution to the `TwoWayProperty` operation. As a result, native two-way DOM bindings were emitted without the required sanitizer function, whereas equivalent one-way bindings would be properly sanitized.\n\nThis flaw enables an attacker who can control the value of a two-way bound sensitive property to bypass Angular's built-in sanitization logic, potentially leading to client-side Cross-Site Scripting (XSS).\n\n### Impact\n\nAny Angular application that uses two-way data binding (`[()]` or `bindon-`) on security-sensitive native DOM properties (like `innerHTML`, `href` on `<a>`, `src` on `<img>`/`<iframe>`, etc.) is vulnerable to this security bypass.\n\nOnce exploited, this allows a malicious actor to supply an unsanitized property binding value that bypasses core sanitization constraints. This could lead to the execution of arbitrary JavaScript within the target user's browser context, potentially resulting in session hijacking, sensitive data exposure, or unauthorized actions on behalf of the user.\n\n### Attack Preconditions\n\nTo successfully exploit this vulnerability, the following environment parameters and application states must concurrently exist:\n\n  1. Two-Way Binding on Sensitive Properties: The application must bind to a sensitive native DOM property using the two-way binding syntax (e.g., `<div [(innerHTML)]=\"userContent\"></div>`).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nBypass via template tag · Advisory · mganss/HtmlSanitizer · GitHub (https://github.com/mganss/HtmlSanitizer/security/advisories/GHSA-j92c-7v7g-gj3f)\n【turn957545search7】 [wordlim: 200] Published: 9 months ago; Crawled: last month; If in addition to the template tag, the `shadowrootmode` attribute is allowed through `sanitizer.AllowedAttributes.Add(\"shadowrootmode\");`, the simple payload of `<div><template shadowrootmode=\"open\"><script>alert('xss')</script>` would bypass the sanitizer.\n\n# Bypass via template tag\n\nModerate\n\nmganss published GHSA-j92c-7v7g-gj3f Feb 3, 2026\n\n## Package\n\nHtmlSanitizer (NuGet)\n\n## Affected versions\n\n< 9.0.892, < 9.1.893-beta\n\n## Patched versions\n\n9.0.892, 9.0.893-beta\n\n## Description\n\n### Impact\n\nIf the `template` tag is allowed, its contents are not sanitized. The `template` tag is a special tag that does not usually render its contents, unless the `shadowrootmode` attribute is set to `open` or `closed`.\n\nThe lack of sanitization of the template tag brings up two bypasses:\n\n  1. it is still possible to forcibly render the contents of a `<template>` tag through mutation XSS. The DOM parsers in browsers such as Chromium have a node depth limit of 512 and tags which are beyond that depth are flattened. This in turn allows elements within `<template>` (which are not sanitized) to be effectively 'popped out'. An example would look like this: `<div>[...]<template><script>alert('xss')</script>` where `[...]` denotes at least another 509 opening `<div>` tags.\n  2. If in addition to the template tag, the `shadowrootmode` attribute is allowed through `sanitizer.AllowedAttributes.Add(\"shadowrootmode\");`, the simple payload of `<div><template shadowrootmode=\"open\"><script>alert('xss')</script>` would bypass the sanitizer. This is because such usage of `<template>` attaches a shadow root to its parent: `<div>`, and its contents will be rendered.\n\nNote that the default configuration is not affected because the `template` tag is disallowed by default.\n\n### Patches\n\nThe problem has been patched in versions 9.0.892 and 9.1.893-beta.\n\n### Workarounds\n\nDisallow the `template` tag. It is disallowed by default.\n\n### Severity\n\nModerate\n\n### CVE ID\n\nCVE-2026-25543\n\n### Weaknesses\n\nNo CWEs\n\n### Credits\n\n  * nsysean Analyst\n\nYou can’t perform that action at this time.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nallowedTags / allowedAttributes bypass leads to permissive sanitization (XSS risk) · Advisory · AhmedAdelFahim/express-xss-sanitizer · GitHub (https://github.com/AhmedAdelFahim/express-xss-sanitizer/security/advisories/GHSA-3843-rr4g-m8jq)\n【turn957545search8】 [wordlim: 200] Published: 7 months ago; Crawled: 4 weeks ago; A vulnerability has been identified in express-xss-sanitizer (<= 2.0.1) where restrictive sanitization configurations are silently ignored. ... This behavior violates the expected API contract and may lead to security issues such as content injection or XSS, depending on how the sanitized output is used.\n\n# allowedTags / allowedAttributes bypass leads to permissive sanitization (XSS risk)\n\nHigh\n\nAhmedAdelFahim published GHSA-3843-rr4g-m8jq Mar 25, 2026\n\n## Package\n\nexpress-xss-sanitizer (npm)\n\n## Affected versions\n\n2.0.1\n\n## Patched versions\n\n2.0.2\n\n## Description\n\n## Description\n\nA vulnerability has been identified in express-xss-sanitizer (<= 2.0.1) where restrictive sanitization configurations are silently ignored.\n\nWhen a developer explicitly sets:\n\nallowedTags: []\nallowedAttributes: {}\n\nthe library incorrectly treats these values as \"not provided\" due to length/emptiness checks, and falls back to sanitize-html's default configuration.\n\nAs a result, instead of stripping all HTML tags and attributes, the sanitizer allows a permissive set of tags ` (e.g., <a>, <p>, <div>, etc.) and attributes (e.g., href on <a>)`.\n\nThis behavior violates the expected API contract and may lead to security issues such as content injection or XSS, depending on how the sanitized output is used.\n\n## Impact\n\nDevelopers intending to fully strip HTML content by providing empty allowedTags or allowedAttributes configurations may unknowingly allow a wide range of HTML elements and attributes.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nIncomplete fix of CVE-2025-66021 — allowed elements inside style/script allowTextIn block bypass AttributePolicy/URL policy, enabling XSS · Advisory · OWASP/java-html-sanitizer · GitHub (https://github.com/OWASP/java-html-sanitizer/security/advisories/GHSA-vqwm-jvq2-mfwc)\n【turn957545search9】 [wordlim: 200] Published: last month; Crawled: last week; For an allowed element it re-emits the entire tag verbatim, never invoking that element's `AttributePolicy` or URL policy — so an allowed element carrying a dangerous attribute (`onerror=…`) or URL (`src=javascript:…`) passes through unfiltered, re-enabling XSS under the exact policy class the original fix protected. ... Applications using a policy that allows `style`/`script` with `allowTextIn` and any attribute-bearing allowed element will pass attacker-controlled event-handler attributes and `javascript:` URLs through the sanitizer, producing stored or reflected XSS in the victim's browser. ... Attack vector\n\n# Incomplete fix of CVE-2025-66021 — allowed elements inside style/script allowTextIn block bypass AttributePolicy/URL policy, enabling XSS\n\nHigh\n\njmanico published GHSA-vqwm-jvq2-mfwc Sep 22, 2026\n\n## Package\n\ncom.googlecode.owasp-java-html-sanitizer:owasp-java-html-sanitizer (Maven)\n\n## Affected versions\n\n< 20260921.1\n\n## Patched versions\n\n20260921.1\n\n## Description\n\n### Affected versions\n\nEvery release before 20260921.1. Releases 20260101.1 through 20260313.1 carry the first fix for CVE-2025-66021, which this report shows was incomplete. Earlier releases are affected by CVE-2025-66021 itself, and the payloads below pass through them as well.\n\nOnly policies that allow a `style` or `script` element and allow text in it with `allowTextIn` are affected. The prepackaged policies in `Sanitizers` are not.\n\n### Patches\n\nFixed in 20260921.1 (#465, #472). Text kept inside a `style` or `script` element, or any other element whose content is written as raw text, no longer carries tags: every start and end tag is removed from it, whether or not the policy allows that element. \n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nMarp Core allows XSS by improper neutralization of HTML sanitization · Advisory · marp-team/marp-core · GitHub (https://github.com/marp-team/marp-core/security/advisories/GHSA-x52f-h5g4-8qv5)\n【turn957545search10】 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.9 years ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; When the allowlist is active, if insufficient HTML comments are included, the sanitizer may fail to properly sanitize HTML content and lead cross-site scripting (XSS). ... Scope: More severe when a scope change occurs, e.g. one vulnerable component impacts resources in components beyond its security scope.\n\n# Marp Core allows XSS by improper neutralization of HTML sanitization\n\nModerate\n\nyhatt published GHSA-x52f-h5g4-8qv5 Dec 26, 2024\n\n## Package\n\n@marp-team/marp-core (npm)\n\n## Affected versions\n\n>= 3.0.2, <= 3.9.0\n\n4.0.0\n\n## Patched versions\n\n3.9.1\n\n4.0.1\n\n## Description\n\nMarp Core (`@marp-team/marp-core`) from v3.0.2 to v3.9.0 and v4.0.0, are vulnerable to cross-site scripting (XSS) due to improper neutralization of HTML sanitization.\n\n### Impact\n\nMarp Core includes an HTML sanitizer with allowlist support. In the affected versions, the built-in allowlist is enabled by default. When the allowlist is active, if insufficient HTML comments are included, the sanitizer may fail to properly sanitize HTML content and lead cross-site scripting (XSS).\n\n### Patches\n\nMarp Core v3.9.1 and v4.0.1 have been patched to fix that.\n\n### Workarounds\n\nIf you are unable to update the package immediately, disable all HTML tags by setting `html: false` option in the `Marp` class constructor.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nXSS via noscript tag and improper sanitization in style tags · Advisory · OWASP/java-html-sanitizer · GitHub (https://github.com/OWASP/java-html-sanitizer/security/advisories/GHSA-g9gq-3pfx-2gw2)\n【turn957545search11】 [wordlim: 200] Published: 11 months ago; Crawled: 3 months ago; It is observed that OWASP java html sanitizer is vulnerable to XSS if HtmlPolicyBuilder allows `noscript` and `style` tags with `allowTextIn` inside the style tag. ... The following condition is very edge case but if we combine a HtmlPolicyBuilder with any other tags except `noscript` and allow `style` tag with `allowTextIn` inside the style tag then In this case sanitizer would be safe from XSS.\n\n# XSS via noscript tag and improper sanitization in style tags\n\nHigh\n\njmanico published GHSA-g9gq-3pfx-2gw2 Nov 25, 2025\n\n## Package\n\nowasp-java-html-sanitizer (Maven)\n\n## Affected versions\n\n20240325.1\n\n## Patched versions\n\nNone\n\n## Description\n\n### Summary\n\nIt is observed that OWASP java html sanitizer is vulnerable to XSS if HtmlPolicyBuilder allows `noscript` and `style` tags with `allowTextIn` inside the style tag. This could lead to XSS if the payload is crafted in such a way that it does not sanitise the CSS and allows tags which is not mentioned in HTML policy.\n\n### Details\n\nThe OWASP java HTML sanitizer is vulnerable to XSS. This only happens when HtmlPolicyBuilder allows `noscript` & `style` tag with `allowTextIn` inside style tags.\n\nThe following condition is very edge case but if we combine a HtmlPolicyBuilder with any other tags except `noscript` and allow `style` tag with `allowTextIn` inside the style tag then In this case sanitizer would be safe from XSS. This happens because how the browser also perceives `noscript` tags post sanitization.\n"}
